<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-ch-03-key-concepts" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Key Concepts</title>

  <p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Heat and temperature are a result of particles randomly moving in all directions.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Temperature is related to the average speed of this movement, whereas thermal energy measures the total energy.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          As heat is transferred into or out of a substance, it may undergo a phase change, such as melting, freezing, boiling, or condensation.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          During processes like melting or boiling (involving an increase in temperature), the phase transition involves energy being used to rip molecules apart, instead of contributing to their movement.
          This energy is called the latent heat.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Thermal energy can be conducted (through direct physical contact), convected (through the movement of fluids), and radiation (through light waves).
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>
</section>
